he box; serve the Svelte frontend from the same domain to keep CORS and deployment simple\" is architecture.\n/workspace/agents/designer.md:69:- Do not propose animations or interactions unless they serve a clear purpose\n/workspace/agents/devops.md:35:- Provisioning and configuration (servers, databases, services)\n/workspace/agents/devops.md:46:- Do not change application behaviour to fit the infrastructure — the pipeline should serve the code, not the other way around\n/workspace/agents/owner.md:3:description: Reviews features and decisions from the product and user perspective. Use when planning new features, evaluating scope, or sanity-checking that what's being built actually serves the end user.\n/workspace/agents/owner.md:30:For solicms specifically: the product exists to serve non-technical content editors at non-profit organisations who are coming from WordPress and found it overwhelming. Radical simplicity is not a nice-to-have — it is the product. Every decision that adds complexity to the admin UI is a step in the wrong direction, regardless of how technically elegant it is.\n/workspace/agents/owner.md:73:- SHIP — this serves the user, fits the product, scope is clear, build it\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:3:description: Manages and administers servers — services, systemd units, logs, TLS, updates, users, and uptime. Use when something needs to be run, maintained, fixed, or audited on a server, especially one you administer directly over SSH.\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:8:You are a senior systems administrator. You manage servers: what runs on them, how it stays up, how it gets patched, and how to fix it when it breaks. Where the developer writes code and the devops engineer ships it, you operate and maintain the machines themselves.\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:10:You are hands-on: you inspect config, run commands, and change what's broken. You work over SSH against the server(s) the request names, from the credentials and access available to you. You never guess your way through an operation you cannot verify — if you lack access or a command's effect is unclear, you say so.\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:40:- Reproducing locally or via the server's own tooling, isolating the failure, fixing the root cause\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:52:2. Inspect the current state — run the commands yourself against the server: status, config, logs\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:61:- Never apply a change to a production server whose effect you cannot verify or roll back\n---\n94:The token is provisioned out-of-band: `mg serve-token` generates a fresh one\n","metadata":{"output":"/workspace/README.md:300:| `mg serve` | the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a control API (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents, health, mutating lifecycle endpoints, and a live session-log SSE stream) over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a native GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or from a VPS; see [Listener](#listener) below |\n/workspace/README.md:610:| `@sysadmin` | Manages and administers servers — services, TLS, updates, uptime | read + write |\n/workspace/README.md:1155:`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a control API over\n/workspace/README.md:1168:# <manigot checkout>/config/serve.json  (override with --registry)\n/workspace/README.md:1172:`name` is required — the URL segment the project is served under, chosen by\n/workspace/README.md:1186:nginx) — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates TLS, the\n/workspace/README.md:1200:`serve.ProjectLocks`:\n/workspace/README.md:1231:is Server-Sent Events over the plain `net/http` server — the network twin of\n/workspace/README.md:1247:root via the `serve.ProjectLocks` pattern — while launch-agent, launch-jdi,\n/workspace/agents/architect.md:40:- Where the system runs: platform choice (PaaS, VPS, serverless, managed services) and why\n/workspace/agents/architect.md:74:Be specific. \"Use a modern framework\" is not architecture. \"Use Laravel for the backend because the team already knows it and it ships auth, migrations, and an ORM out of the box; serve the Svelte frontend from the same domain to keep CORS and deployment simple\" is architecture.\n/workspace/agents/designer.md:69:- Do not propose animations or interactions unless they serve a clear purpose\n/workspace/agents/devops.md:35:- Provisioning and configuration (servers, databases, services)\n/workspace/agents/devops.md:46:- Do not change application behaviour to fit the infrastructure — the pipeline should serve the code, not the other way around\n/workspace/agents/owner.md:3:description: Reviews features and decisions from the product and user perspective. Use when planning new features, evaluating scope, or sanity-checking that what's being built actually serves the end user.\n/workspace/agents/owner.md:30:For solicms specifically: the product exists to serve non-technical content editors at non-profit organisations who are coming from WordPress and found it overwhelming. Radical simplicity is not a nice-to-have — it is the product. Every decision that adds complexity to the admin UI is a step in the wrong direction, regardless of how technically elegant it is.\n/workspace/agents/owner.md:73:- SHIP — this serves the user, fits the product, scope is clear, build it\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:3:description: Manages and administers servers — services, systemd units, logs, TLS, updates, users, and uptime. Use when something needs to be run, maintained, fixed, or audited on a server, especially one you administer directly over SSH.\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:8:You are a senior systems administrator. You manage servers: what runs on them, how it stays up, how it gets patched, and how to fix it when it breaks. Where the developer writes code and the devops engineer ships it, you operate and maintain the machines themselves.\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:10:You are hands-on: you inspect config, run commands, and change what's broken. You work over SSH against the server(s) the request names, from the credentials and access available to you. You never guess your way through an operation you cannot verify — if you lack access or a command's effect is unclear, you say so.\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:40:- Reproducing locally or via the server's own tooling, isolating the failure, fixing the root cause\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:52:2. Inspect the current state — run the commands yourself against the server: status, config, logs\n/workspace/agents/sysadmin.md:61:- Never apply a change to a production server whose effect you cannot verify or roll back\n---\n94:The token is provisioned out-of-band: `mg serve-token` generates a fresh one\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"serve\" /workspace/README.md /workspace/agents/*.md 2>/dev/null | grep -vi \"observer\\|preserved\\|deserve\" | head -30; echo ---; grep -n \"serve-token\\|serve-projects\" /workspace/project-template/docs/AGENTS.md | head","time":{"start":1787992699225,"end":1787992699228}},"id":"prt_04cab7144001nAdU7f2GnD2q7g","sessionID":"ses_fb356b356ffep6vyvmzQINQBoz","messageID":"msg_04cab5e56001U40va1RcAU1XMH"}}
